### 2. Multi-Domain Control Plane Framework
- **Presentation**: [02) A Control Plane Framework for Multi-Domain Deterministic Networking](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-detnet-02-a-control-plane-framework-for-multi-domain-deterministic-networking-00)
- **Presenter**: Carlos J. Bernardos
- **Draft**: `draft-ietf-detnet-multi-domain-framework`
- **Discussion**:
  - Carlos J. Bernardos summarized updates in versions -02 and -03, which addressed scope clarification (focusing on controller domains), refined definitions pointing to existing RFCs, and added Abstraction and Control of TE Networks (ACTN) alongside hierarchical PCE as a second realization of the hierarchical model.
  - Lou Berger questioned whether the framework should remain limited to hierarchical controller models or explicitly include distributed control. Carlos J. Bernardos clarified that peering models are included, and agreed to review the document alongside Lou Berger to ensure hierarchical and peering/distributed models are clearly presented as equivalent options.

### 3. SRv6 Data Plane for Service Protection
- **Presentation**: [03) Update on SRv6 Data Plane for Service Protection and Implementation](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-detnet-03-update-on-srv6-data-plane-for-service-protection-and-implementation-00)
- **Presenter**: Ferenc Fejes
- **Draft**: `draft-ietf-detnet-srv6-svc-protection`
- **Discussion**:
  - Technical content is stable and aligned with the SPRING Working Group.
  - Open-source implementations (Dependable Networking Toolkit and TSN/SRv6 PREOF) were updated to support flexible tcpdump/Wireshark-like syntax for flow filtering by executing classic BPF compiled code inside extended BPF (eBPF).
  - Planned next steps include defining redundancy SID address designs, updating redundancy policies for DetNet scenarios, and documenting operational considerations for coexisting with other SRv6 services.

### 4. Dataplane Enhancement Taxonomy
- **Presentation**: [04) Dataplane Enhancement Taxonomy](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-detnet-04-dataplane-enhancement-taxonomy-00)
- **Presenter**: Jinou Joung
- **Draft**: `draft-ietf-detnet-dataplane-taxonomy`
- **Discussion**:
  - Version -07 added a third reference topology (RT3: Hybrid Leaf-Spine and Tree) representing medium-scale enterprise/industrial networks (such as AI inference and industrial automation), as well as Section 8.2 covering category interoperability based on feedback from Mike Blanche.
  - Lou Berger and János Farkas discussed the publication process, noting taxonomy and framework drafts are traditionally held while underlying data plane solutions mature. Toerless Eckert suggested soliciting early reviews from other areas; Lou Berger agreed to request a Transport Area (TSV) early review.

### 5. Latency Guarantee with Stateless Fair Queuing (NSCORE)
- **Presentation**: [05) Latency Guarantee with Stateless Fair Queuing](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-detnet-05-latency-guarantee-with-stateless-fair-queuing-00)
- **Presenter**: Jinou Joung
- **Draft**: `draft-ietf-detnet-stateless-fair-queuing`
- **Discussion**:
  - Version -02 updated IPv6 Hop-by-Hop Options header alignment to 8 octets per RFC 8200 and updated MPLS MNA post-stack header formats to match `draft-ietf-mpls-mna-detnet`.
  - Addressed coordination with ITU-T SG13 Recommendations Y.3129 and Y.3148 regarding metadata definitions. Jinou Joung confirmed he will initiate revisions in ITU-T to align the recommendations with the IETF draft. Lou Berger emphasized that IETF and ITU-T specifications must remain synchronized.

### 6. TCQF and gLBF Data Plane Enhancements
- **Presentation**: [06) TCQF, gLBF update](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-detnet-06-tcqf-glbf-update-00)
- **Presenter**: Toerless Eckert
- **Drafts**: `draft-ietf-detnet-tcqf`, `draft-ietf-detnet-glbf`
- **Discussion**:
  - Integrated structural feedback from Florian Kauer and technical details from Donald Eastlake. Updated IEEE 802.1Q-2022 section references.
  - **Draft Naming**: Discussed naming alternatives to clarify "tagged CQF" (e.g., "cycle-tagged CQF" / cTCQF or "iCQF").
  - **Pseudocode**: Toerless Eckert defended keeping formal forwarding pseudocode in data plane specifications based on RFC precedents in BIER and SRv6. Lou Berger advised ensuring clear distinction between normative text and informative pseudocode to prevent implementation ambiguities.
  - **Encapsulations**: IP/DSCP remains the primary normative encapsulation, while other existing deployment encapsulations will be documented informatively in appendixes.

### 7. PIFO and NSCORE Updates and Hardware Implementation
- **Presentation**: [07) PIFO and NSCORE update](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-detnet-07-pifo-and-nscore-update-00)
- **Presenter**: Yong-Geun Hong
- **Drafts**: `draft-ietf-detnet-ontime-forwarding`, `draft-ietf-detnet-nscore`
- **Discussion**:
  - `draft-ietf-detnet-ontime-forwarding` (v02) added mathematical proof supporting the solution.
  - `draft-ietf-detnet-nscore` (v02) introduced an arrival-time-based scheduling method eliminating the need for core nodes to maintain inter-node delay state.
  - Presented FPGA hardware prototype details demonstrating On-Time Forwarding (PIFO) and NSCORE running on a unified PIFO queue architecture supporting both work-conserving and non-work-conserving modes. Ketan Talaulikar inquired about node ordering; Yong-Geun Hong clarified that elimination occurs at ingress while sequence ordering occurs at egress.

### 8. PREOF Observability and OAM Requirements
- **Presentation**: [08) PREOF Observability and OAM Requirements](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-detnet-08-preof-observability-and-oam-requirements-00)
- **Presenter**: Luis M. Contreras
- **Draft**: Individual draft (`draft-contreras-detnet-preof-oam-requirements`)
- **Discussion**:
  - Outlined updated OAM requirements for Packet Replication, Elimination, and Ordering Functions (PREOF). Added four new requirements focusing on correlation across observation points, aggregated reporting (counters/statistics rather than per-packet logging) to ensure scalability, rate-limiting observability output, and distinguishing packet drops from duplicate eliminations.
  - Ketan Talaulikar suggested signaling expected packet copy counts to elimination points to reduce unnecessary notification traffic. Toerless Eckert highlighted tracking sequence window edges to detect path shifts. János Farkas recommended aligning terminology with IEEE 802.1CB maintenance points and leveraging existing OAM tools. Lou Berger requested alignment with existing DetNet OAM RFCs and working group drafts.

### 9. Applicability of ACTN to Multi-Domain Deterministic Networking
- **Presentation**: [09) Applicability of ACTN to Multi-Domain Deterministic Networking](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-detnet-09-applicability-of-actn-to-multi-domain-deterministic-networking-00)
- **Presenter**: Luis M. Contreras
- **Draft**: Individual draft (`draft-contreras-detnet-actn-multidomain-applicability`)
- **Discussion**:
  - Explored mapping Abstraction and Control of TE Networks (ACTN, RFC 8453) components—Customer Network Controller (CNC), Multi-Domain Service Controller (MDSC), and Provisioning Network Controller (PNC)—to multi-domain DetNet scenarios (e.g., hybrid wireless/wired and TSN/IP-MPLS domains).
  - Raised open questions regarding minimum domain exposure, SLO coordination, and controller responsibility division. Lou Berger suggested shifting generic controller architecture questions from this document into `draft-ietf-detnet-multi-domain-framework`.

### 10. Multipath Traffic Engineering for Reliable and Available Wireless DetNet
- **Presentation**: [10 ) Multipath Traffic Engineering for Reliable and Available Wireless DetNet](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-detnet-10-multipath-traffic-engineering-for-reliable-and-available-wireless-detnet-01)
- **Presenter**: Ketan Talaulikar
- **Draft**: Individual draft (`draft-talaulikar-detnet-mrte`)
- **Discussion**:
  - Introduced Multipath Traffic Engineering (MPTE/MRTE) for wireless DetNet and RAW (Reliable and Available Wireless). Uses Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) with junction nodes providing bounded replication, elimination, and local repair (PLR using a DLEP fast loop and controller slow loop).
  - Lou Berger clarified that RAW DAG junctions do not strictly require packet replication; single-path selection based on link quality dynamically satisfies the architecture. Jinou Joung noted that replication increases network utilization and requested controller-side optimization. Lou Berger and Toerless Eckert emphasized that wireless/RAW capabilities are fully integrated components of the overarching DetNet architecture.

